//? Title: Chinese Remainders: Compatible Congruences and Checked Periods
//? Subtitle: Actual merge certificates, non-coprime moduli and fixed-width limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Several remainder measurements may describe one unknown integer, but they can
also contradict each other. Generalised Chinese remainder reconstruction finds
the unique compatible class modulo the least common multiple, or detects a
contradiction. We follow actual sequential merges for a small example, retain
the original coprime residue grid and non-coprime controls, and enumerate the
fraction of compatible residue pairs. The canonical C++ solver records its
Bezout coefficients and wide candidates, checks periods before narrowing, and
distinguishes mathematical inconsistency from a fixed-width range failure.
)

\toc

> A small reconstruction, one merge at a time

Find an integer with remainders 2, 3 and 2 modulo 3, 5 and 7 respectively.
Starting from every integer, represented by 0 modulo 1, the first condition
leaves 2 modulo 3. The second leaves 8 modulo 15: 8 has remainders 2 and 3.
The third leaves 23 modulo 105. These are actual accumulator states from the
native solver, rather than a second implementation of its merge rule.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/crt_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::chinese_remainder_trace({2,3,2},{3,5,7});
  if(trace.status!=dm::algorithms::CrtStatus::Complete || !trace.solution || trace.solution->remainder!=23 || trace.solution->modulus!=105)return 1;
  std::print("| merge | before | target | gcd | Bezout P,Q | t | candidate |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& s:trace.steps)
    std::print("| {} | {} mod {} | {} mod {} | {} | {},{} | {} | {} mod {} |\n",s.index+1,s.before.remainder,s.before.modulus,
        s.normalized_remainder,s.input_modulus,s.gcd,dm::algorithms::int128_text(s.bezout_first),dm::algorithms::int128_text(s.bezout_second),
        s.multiplier,dm::algorithms::int128_text(s.candidate_remainder),dm::algorithms::int128_text(s.candidate_modulus));
  std::print("\nActual attempts: {}. Euclidean quotient/remainder steps: {}.\n",trace.work.attempted_merges,trace.work.euclidean_steps);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(60,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=5.1;node.height=2.2;node.font_size=20;
  const std::array<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint,4> positions{{{0,0},{8,0},{8,-4},{0,-4}}};
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<=trace.steps.size();++i) {
    const auto state=i<trace.steps.size()?trace.steps[i].before:trace.prefix;
    if(i==trace.steps.size())node.fill={220,252,231};
    const auto id=std::format("state{}",i);
    diagram.node(id,positions[i],std::format("prefix {}\nx = {} mod {}",i,state.remainder,state.modulus),node);
    if(i) {
      const auto& step=trace.steps[i-1];
      diagram.edge(std::format("state{}",i-1),id,std::format("add {} mod {}",step.normalized_remainder,step.input_modulus));
    }
  }
  diagram.text({4,2.3},"Actual CRT states: retain one compatible residue class",20)
      .text({4,-6.3},"Final class: 23 + 105k, for every integer k",19).save_svg("build/crt_method.svg");
}
```
