| ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- |
| 1 | 0 mod 1 | 2 mod 3 | 1 | 1,0 | 2 | 2 mod 3 |
| 2 | 2 mod 3 | 3 mod 5 | 1 | 2,-1 | 2 | 8 mod 15 |
| 3 | 8 mod 15 | 2 mod 7 | 1 | 1,-2 | 1 | 23 mod 105 |

Actual attempts: 3. Euclidean quotient/remainder steps: 8.
\image(figures/crt_method.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Three actual merges reconstruct 23 modulo 105; the table retains the coefficients used by the kernel.)
\alttext(Four boxes show 0 modulo 1, 2 modulo 3, 8 modulo 15 and 23 modulo 105. Arrows add one congruence at a time.)

> Deriving the general merge

Suppose the current class is x=R modulo M and the next target is x=r modulo
m, with positive moduli and canonical remainders. Every member of the first
class has the form x=R+Mt. The new condition becomes Mt=r−R modulo m.
Let g=gcd(M,m), and obtain the actual Bezout coefficients P and Q from the
extended Euclidean algorithm:

$$
MP+mQ=g.
$$
\alttext(M times P plus m times Q equals their greatest common divisor g.)

A solution exists exactly when g divides r−R. Necessity follows because g
divides both M and m. When divisibility holds, divide the identity by g to
make P an inverse of M/g modulo m/g. Then choose

$$
t=\left(\frac{r-R}{g}P\right)\bmod(m/g),\qquad
L=M(m/g),\qquad R'=(R+Mt)\bmod L.
$$
\alttext(The correction t is the divided remainder difference times the Bezout coefficient, reduced modulo m divided by g. The merged period L is the least common multiple, and the new remainder is R plus M times t reduced modulo L.)

This construction satisfies both congruences. The difference between any two
solutions is divisible by M and m, hence by L=lcm(M,m). The class is therefore
unique modulo L. Applying the argument inductively proves the sequential
solver correct whenever each compatible intermediate period is representable.
An inconsistent prefix proves the whole system inconsistent. A range failure
alone gives no such verdict about unvisited constraints.

The second merge above has M=3, m=5, P=2, Q=−1 and r−R=1, so t=2 and
R'=2+3·2=8. The third has M=15, m=7, P=1 and Q=−2. Its difference is −6,
so t=−6 modulo 7=1 and R'=8+15=23. Non-coprime moduli use the same rule;
the period is their least common multiple rather than their product.

For an arbitrary collection, compatibility is equivalent to

$$
\gcd(m_i,m_j)\mid(r_i-r_j)\quad\hbox{for every pair }i,j.
$$
\alttext(Every pair of input remainders must agree modulo the greatest common divisor of its moduli.)

Necessity follows by subtracting any two congruences. For sufficiency, consider
each prime separately and select an input containing its largest power among
the moduli. Pairwise agreement forces every smaller power to have the same
residue. The selected prime powers are mutually coprime, so the coprime theorem
combines them into one class modulo their product, which is the full least
common multiple. This argument establishes the mathematical condition without
requiring factorisation in the implementation.

> The original controls and the residue grid

For x=2 modulo 6 and x=8 modulo 12, the difference 6 is divisible by
gcd(6,12)=6, giving 8 modulo 12. For x=1 modulo 2 and x=0 modulo 4,
the difference −1 is not divisible by 2, so no integer works.
The following program retains these controls and the original coprime grid.
Every cell calls the native solver for its displayed pair of remainders.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chinese_remainder_grid.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <set>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<std::int64_t>> residues{{2,3,2},{2,8},{1,0}};
  const std::vector<std::vector<std::int64_t>> moduli{{3,5,7},{6,12},{2,4}};
  const std::array<std::string,3> names{"classic","compatible non-coprime","inconsistent"};
  std::print("| system | solvable | remainder | period | representative satisfies input |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<names.size();++i) {
    const auto s=dm::algorithms::chinese_remainder(residues[i],moduli[i]);
    const auto check=s.solvable?dm::algorithms::crt_residue_satisfies(s.remainder,residues[i],moduli[i]):false;
    if(s.solvable && !check)return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",names[i],s.solvable,s.remainder,s.modulus,s.solvable?(check?"true":"false"):"not applicable");
  }
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1000);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=1.15;node.height=0.95;node.font_size=22;
  std::set<std::int64_t> representatives;
  for(std::int64_t a=0;a<3;++a) {
    diagram.text({-1.6,2.0*a},std::format("a={}",a),20);
    for(std::int64_t b=0;b<5;++b) {
      const auto s=dm::algorithms::chinese_remainder({a,b},{3,5});
      representatives.insert(s.remainder);node.fill=(a==2 && b==3)?dm::graphics::RGBA{254,240,138}:dm::graphics::RGBA{219,234,254};
      diagram.node(std::format("cell{}_{}",a,b),{2.0*b,2.0*a},std::format("{}",s.remainder),node);
    }
  }
  if(representatives.size()!=15)return 1;
  for(int b=0;b<5;++b)diagram.text({2.0*b,5.5},std::format("b={}",b),20);
  diagram.text({4,7},"Coprime residues: one x for each pair (a,b)",21)
      .text({4,-1.6},"a = x mod 3, b = x mod 5; highlighted cell: x = 8 mod 15",18)
      .save_svg("build/chinese_remainder_grid.svg");
  std::print("\nDistinct canonical representatives in the grid: {} of 15.\n",representatives.size());
}
```
