Distinct canonical representatives in the grid: 15 of 15.
\image(figures/chinese_remainder_grid.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original three controls and the bijection between fifteen coprime residue pairs and fifteen classes modulo 15.)
\alttext(Columns are remainders zero through four modulo five; rows are remainders zero through two modulo three. Each cell gives its unique representative from zero through fourteen. The highlighted pair two and three gives eight.)

The map x modulo 15 to (x modulo 3,x modulo 5) is a bijection. This is the
coprime Chinese remainder theorem. With non-coprime moduli, some target pairs
are incompatible: the two components must agree modulo their shared gcd.

> Counting compatible pairs

Among the m·n canonical pairs, each of the g=gcd(m,n) common residue classes
contains (m/g)(n/g) pairs whose components agree modulo g. Therefore

$$
N_{\rm compatible}=g(m/g)(n/g)=mn/g=\operatorname{lcm}(m,n),\qquad
\Pr(\hbox{compatible})=1/g.
$$
\alttext(The number of compatible canonical pairs is the least common multiple of the moduli. For uniformly enumerated pairs, the compatible fraction is one divided by their greatest common divisor.)

This is an exact finite count, not a probabilistic model for arbitrary input
data. We enumerate every pair for five small modulus choices, compare actual
solver verdicts with the formula, and plot observed fractions against 1/g.
There is no natural observational dataset for these arithmetic identities.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/crt_compatibility.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numeric>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::array<std::pair<std::int64_t,std::int64_t>,5> pairs{{{3,5},{4,6},{6,12},{8,12},{12,18}}};
  std::vector<double> x,observed,theory;std::vector<std::string> labels;
  std::print("| moduli | gcd | all pairs | compatible pairs | predicted lcm | fraction |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto [m,n]:pairs) {
    std::int64_t compatible=0;const auto g=std::gcd(m,n);
    for(std::int64_t a=0;a<m;++a)for(std::int64_t b=0;b<n;++b)
      compatible+=dm::algorithms::chinese_remainder({a,b},{m,n}).solvable;
    if(compatible*g!=m*n)return 1;
    x.push_back(x.size());observed.push_back(double(compatible)/(m*n));theory.push_back(1.0/g);labels.push_back(std::format("{}/{}",m,n));
    std::print("| {}/{} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} |\n",m,n,g,m*n,compatible,std::lcm(m,n),observed.back());
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.bars(x,observed,"enumeration",{37,99,235}).points(x,theory,"1/g",{234,88,12},6)
      .x_tick_labels(labels).legend({"enumerated fraction","1/g"},{{37,99,235},{234,88,12}})
      .title("Compatible pairs: shared factors impose constraints")
      .x_label("modulus pair").y_label("compatible fraction").y_limits(0,1.3)
      .save_svg("build/crt_compatibility.svg");
}
```
