\caption(Actual kernel counters determine exact caps; validation, empty input and unit moduli have explicit contracts.)

For n inputs, full validation costs O(n) visits. Each attempted merge performs
an extended gcd and a constant number of widened arithmetic operations; its
division count is logarithmic in the positive modulus magnitudes. The ordinary
solver uses constant numeric working state beyond the borrowed input. The
trace owns O(a) merge records for a attempts, including the first rejected
attempt. These are operation counts for bounded integer arithmetic, not a
bit-complexity analysis for unbounded integers or a wall-clock benchmark.
Calls have independent state; inputs are borrowed without mutation and trace
records own their values.

The native tests independently enumerate 12,286 small two- and three-condition
systems and compare all verdicts, periods and representatives with a brute-force
oracle. They check each actual Bezout identity, candidate and counter. Another
1,000 signed random systems and 1,000 constructed near-limit systems cover
normalisation and representable periods. Wide correction products, Fibonacci
Euclidean chains, first-overflow versus first-contradiction ordering, exact and
one-below caps, malformed trailing input, owned traces and 100,000 unit
constraints exercise the boundaries. Existing reconstruction and inconsistent
random tests remain. All 24 CRT tests pass with AddressSanitizer and UBSan.

> What the reconstruction establishes

Compatible congruences determine one class modulo the least common multiple.
The gcd test explains non-coprime failure, and the residue-pair census verifies
its exact compatible fraction. Actual merge certificates expose how the solver
reaches its answer. Checked narrowing prevents a wide compatible period from
becoming a wrapped 64-bit result; range failure, work-budget failure and a
mathematical contradiction remain separate outcomes.

> References

- Victor Shoup (2008), *A Computational Introduction to Number Theory and Algebra*,
  version 2.5, sections 2.3–2.4 on congruences and Chinese remainders, and 4.3 on
  computing inverses and Chinese remainders. https://www.shoup.net/ntb/ntb-v2_5.pdf
  The report derives the sequential gcd-compatible extension and documents the
  library's bounded-integer checks separately.
